# sudowhat PAM config. The Touch ID prompt itself is rendered post-PAM by the
# sudo approval plugin loaded from /etc/sudo.conf. A non-console caller is NEVER
# shown that biometric sheet (it would render on the console user's screen); the
# only choice is password vs. denial, made by which variant of this file you
# install.
#
# Apple's openpam fork does not parse the Linux-PAM bracket-list syntax
# `[success=done default=die]`, so the fail-closed semantics use simple flags.
#
# DEFAULT variant (below) — non-console callers get a native password:
#
#   requisite  pam_sudowhat.so                - integrity; failure aborts sudo
#   sufficient pam_sudowhat.so console-gate    - PAM_SUCCESS iff the caller is the
#                                                local console (GUI) user, so the
#                                                console user is never asked for a
#                                                password (Touch ID gates them in
#                                                the approval plugin). For a
#                                                non-console caller it FAILS, so the
#                                                parent /etc/pam.d/sudo chain falls
#                                                through to pam_smartcard /
#                                                pam_opendirectory on the caller's
#                                                own terminal, and the approval
#                                                plugin steps aside for them.
auth    requisite     /usr/local/lib/pam/pam_sudowhat.so
auth    sufficient    /usr/local/lib/pam/pam_sudowhat.so console-gate

# DENY variant — to lock sudo to the local console only (so a stolen password
# alone cannot escalate remotely), replace the `console-gate` line above with:
#
#   auth    sufficient    pam_permit.so
#
# That terminates the auth chain with no password path, so the approval plugin
# denies every non-console caller.
